/*!
    \page tutorial-game-core.html
    \title Tutorial 5: A Game Core Like an MMORPG Server
    \previouspage {Tutorial 4: Scenes and the Game Window}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 6: Connecting the Core to QML}

    All rules live in a C++ core without Qt. It works like the server of an online RPG such
    as \l {https://github.com/otland/forgottenserver}{The Forgotten Server}.

    \b {Why not in QML?} A usual Felgo game would use a \c Timer and JavaScript. That is
    faster to write. We use a separate core because it can be tested without a window, it
    treats the player and the AI exactly the same, and it can move to a real server later.

    \section1 One game thread

    \l game_t runs the game in its own thread. QML never touches the game state. It only
    sends commands:

    \quotefromfile backend/game.h
    \skipto void request_spawn_creature(
    \printuntil void request_set_time_scale

    Example from the bridge:

    \code
    game_.request_walk_to(creature_id, position_t{column, row});
    \endcode

    Each call only queues a task. The game thread runs the tasks one by one, so the game
    logic needs no locks.

    \section1 Dispatcher and scheduler

    \list
        \li \l game_event_dispatcher_t runs tasks in the order they arrive.
        \li \l game_scheduler_t keeps tasks for a point in time: the game tick, income,
            production, research, wildlife.
    \endlist

    \quotefromfile backend/game_scheduler.h
    \skipto class game_scheduler_t
    \printuntil std::vector<std::function<void()>> take_due

    Example: run something in two seconds and cancel it again:

    \code
    const auto id = scheduler_.schedule(now + std::chrono::seconds{2}, [](auto time) {
        // happens two seconds later
    });
    scheduler_.cancel(id);
    \endcode

    In the game loop, due events go to the dispatcher. The scheduler never runs game code
    itself, like in TFS:

    \quotefromfile backend/game.cpp
    \skipto collect_actions();
    \printuntil dispatcher_.dispatch_pending();

    \section1 Game time

    \l game_clock_t turns real time into game time. Pause stops it, 2x and 5x speed it up.
    Scheduled events just follow.

    \section1 Rules as data

    Numbers live in \c data, not in code:

    \quotefromfile data/economy.json
    \skipto "income": {
    \printuntil "levelIncomeBonusPercent"

    Change a value, restart the game, done. No rebuild.
*/
